+
+# Decision: Concurrent Edit Policy for Visit Records
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers on shared tablets correct the same plot visit independently, sometimes days apart, and tablets can be offline for 48 hours. The record store must define what happens when two or more edits derived from the same visit state arrive: which edit becomes the current record, who decides, and what the losers' data looks like afterward. This choice determines the submission envelope tablet-sync stores and transmits, the shape of review-export's coordinator interface, and which visits are eligible for the Friday CSV.
+
+Every option must respect an inherited constraint from the HLD tenets: no submitted edit is silently discarded, and the path from a submitted observation to an exported value stays inspectable. All original submissions are retained regardless of policy.
+
+The scientific content of a visit is not a set of independent fields. A revised species identification and its cover estimate belong together; an observer-name correction and a new species entry do not. The server cannot infer these relationships from field names alone — only the workers and a reviewing coordinator can judge which field changes belong together.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates:**
+
+- **No silent loss** (inherited, HLD tenet): an option that overwrites or discards a submitted edit without a human seeing it is eliminated. This eliminates naive last-write-wins, the spreadsheet-import behavior the team is replacing.
+
+**Weighted criteria:**
+
+- **Scientific coherence** — major. The policy must not produce a current record that combines field changes in ways no worker submitted or reviewed. A visit's fields carry relationships the server cannot see; combining an identification change from one worker with a cover estimate from another can produce a scientifically meaningless record without anyone noticing. (Turns on the HLD tenet of an inspectable path from submitted observation to exported value: a record that is nobody's submission is the least explainable artifact the system could export.)
+- **Review workload** — major. Two coordinators resolve questions in spare time; the policy sets the volume of work that lands on them each week and before each Friday export. (Turns on the HLD tenet of workflows two coordinators can operate without a systems administrator.)
+- **Maintenance burden** — moderate. Rules that combine changes across submissions must be written, tested, and maintained over seasons by a team with no dedicated engineer.
+- **Tablet storage and submission size** — moderate. Tablets hold drafts through restarts and 48-hour disconnections; a full visit payload is small (a few KB), so whole-visit submissions are cheap, but the policy determines what per-visit bookkeeping the tablet must retain and how much of it survives a reinstall.
+- **Export readiness** — moderate. The sooner edits settle into an exportable current record, the more complete the Friday CSV. Policies that channel more visits through review hold more visits out of export.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Naive last-write-wins
+
+**Description.** Each accepted submission simply replaces the current record; the most recently arrived payload wins and earlier edits are invisible to the system afterward.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- No silent loss: **eliminated.** This is the behavior that motivated the project — overwritten corrections with no record of what was lost.
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Every submission carries the complete visit payload and names the base revision it was edited from. Each accepted submission becomes a revision of the visit. When the base is the current revision, the submission becomes the new current revision; when the base is any other revision, the submission is retained as a competing revision and the visit is flagged unresolved until a coordinator selects one candidate — the current revision or any competitor — as the new current revision. No submitted payload is ever combined with another, deleted, or overwritten.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A and worker B both fetch revision 3. A submits revision 4 (base 3), which becomes current. B submits revision 5 (base 3): retained as a competitor, visit unresolved, held out of the Friday CSV until a coordinator selects revision 4 or 5. Both payloads remain in history with their authors and receipt times.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- No silent loss: **passes.** Every submission is retained and surfaces either as the current revision or as a review candidate.
+- Scientific coherence: **strong.** The current record is always exactly one worker's complete submission or a coordinator's explicit selection of one — fields are never combined by rules.
+- Review workload: **weak.** Every concurrent edit — including trivially unrelated ones, such as an observer-name fix alongside a new species entry — creates coordinator work.
+- Maintenance burden: **strong.** No merge rules to write or maintain; the mechanism is revision bookkeeping plus a selection operation.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **strong.** The tablet stores the visit payload it already holds plus one revision ID per visit; submissions are the complete payload it already has.
+- Export readiness: **weak.** Concurrently edited visits stay out of the export until resolved.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a larger review queue and slower export readiness for zero merge logic and a current record that is always someone's actual submission.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission carries a base snapshot and structured per-field changes. The server merges changes to disjoint fields automatically; only overlapping field changes require review. Review and export carry field-level provenance showing which worker contributed each exported value.
+
+**Demonstration.** A's submission changes the observer list; B's changes a species cover estimate. The changes touch no common field, so the server combines them into a new current revision without review. Had both revised the same cover estimate, the overlap would route to a coordinator.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- No silent loss: **passes.** All submissions are retained; provenance records every value's origin.
+- Scientific coherence: **weak.** Disjoint-field combination is exactly where scientific relationships hide: a revision of a species identification in one submission and a revised cover estimate for that species in another are disjoint to the merge rules but scientifically inseparable. The merged record was submitted by nobody and reviewed by nobody.
+- Review workload: **strong.** Only overlapping changes reach the coordinator; most concurrent edits settle without review.
+- Maintenance burden: **weak.** Merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries must be authored, tested against seasonal edge cases, and maintained. A "require manual approval of every merged record" mitigates the coherence weakness but reopens most of the review-queue cost, and the rules still require maintenance.
+- Tablet storage and submission size: **partial.** The tablet must retain base snapshots and compute structured changes rather than submitting its current draft; the machinery is heavier, though payload sizes are comparable.
+- Export readiness: **strong.** Most merged visits reach an exportable current record without coordinator involvement.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a small review queue and fast export readiness for maintained merge rules and current records that may be server-authored combinations.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The decision turns on scientific coherence (major) over review workload and export readiness (major and moderate, both favoring the merge). Merge's strongest honest case is real: it would settle most concurrent edits automatically and keep the coordinator queue short. It is still the right approach to reconsider if the review volume proves unsustainable — for example, if concurrent edits to the same visit turn out to be frequent enough that the two coordinators cannot clear the queue before the Friday export.
+
+It loses here because the case for merge rests on the merge rules correctly distinguishing unrelated field edits, and the relationships that distinction depends on are scientific judgments the rules cannot make: disjoint fields can carry related meaning, and an automatically combined record is a value no worker submitted and no coordinator reviewed — the one artifact the HLD's inspectability tenet cannot explain. The team accepts a larger, more explicit review queue over a smaller invisible one. Maintenance burden (moderate) reinforces the choice: the rules the merge option must maintain are the same rules whose judgments are in doubt.
+
+Committing to whole-visit revisions obligates the downstream segments:
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain and persist the base revision ID of every visit state a worker edits, transmit it with each complete-payload submission (alongside the visit UUID and idempotency key), keep that bookkeeping across restarts and reinstalls, and explain server outcomes to the worker — including that a submission on a stale base was not lost but is awaiting coordinator selection rather than having become current.
+- **Review-export** must present competing revisions for side-by-side comparison of complete visit payloads, let a coordinator select one candidate as current, hold unresolved visits out of the Friday CSV, and surface each visit's resolution history (original submissions, resolutions, selecting coordinator).
